{0 awskit}

Pure AWS infrastructure for credentials, regions, endpoints, structured
errors, HTTP metadata, body metadata, request signing, and runtime adapters.

The core {!module:Awskit} library has no IO dependencies. Concurrency,
networking, filesystem access, clocks, and environment variables live in
adapter libraries that you choose at the application edge.

Service packages such as {{:../awskit-s3/index.html}[awskit-s3]} build on the
runtime abstraction defined here instead of depending directly on Eio, Lwt, or
Unix.

{1 Libraries}

{2 awskit}

The entrypoint is {!module:Awskit}.

Core modules:

{ul
  {li {!module:Awskit.Credentials} — opaque AWS access keys and session tokens}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Region} — AWS region names with result/exn
    constructors}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Endpoint} — scheme, host, port, authority, and URL
    helpers}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Body} — runtime-neutral upload and payload-hash
    metadata}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Request} — body-free HTTP request metadata}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Response} — body-free HTTP response metadata and header
    helpers}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Error} — structured errors and retry classification}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Retry} — shared retry policy used by service packages}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Signing} — pure AWS Signature Version 4 signing}
  {li {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} — runtime abstraction used by service
    packages}
}

{2 awskit-eio}

[Awskit_eio] is the direct-style Eio runtime adapter. Its monad type
is [type 'a t = 'a]. Response bodies are scoped to [with_response] and
[with_download_body].

{2 awskit-lwt}

[Awskit_lwt] is a generic Lwt runtime functor over
[Cohttp_lwt.S.Client]. Use it for custom Lwt HTTP backends.

{2 awskit-lwt-unix}

[Awskit_lwt_unix] is the ready-to-use Lwt + Unix runtime adapter. It
can load credentials from standard AWS environment variables, shared AWS
profile files, ECS/container metadata, or EC2 instance metadata, and region from
standard AWS environment variables when arguments are omitted.

{2 awskit-unix}

[Awskit_unix] contains Unix-specific credential and region sources. It
does not perform HTTP calls.

{1 Minimal Signing Example}

{[
let credentials =
  Awskit.Credentials.create_exn
    ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
    ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
    ()
in
let region = Awskit.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1" in
let payload_hash = Awskit.Body.Payload_hash.sha256_of_string "" in
Awskit.Signing.sign_request
  ~credentials
  ~region
  ~service:"s3"
  ~method_:`GET
  ~path:"/my-key"
  ~query:""
  ~headers:[ ("host", "my-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com") ]
  ~payload_hash
  ~now:Ptime.epoch
]}

Signing is pure and testable without network access. Service packages sign
requests automatically; use {!module:Awskit.Signing} directly for custom AWS
services or low-level tests.

{1 Minimal Eio Runtime Example}

{[
Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
let region = Awskit.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1" in
let conn =
  Awskit_eio.create
    ~env
    ~sw
    ~region
    ~credentials
    ()
in
ignore conn
]}

{1 Minimal Lwt Unix Runtime Example}

{[
let conn =
  Awskit_lwt_unix.create ()
  |> Result.get_ok
in
ignore conn
]}

When omitted, [awskit-lwt-unix] reads:

{[
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
AWS_REGION
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
]}

